Reference points. The player origin is the bottom-center of the collision box. Heights are measured from the surface the player stands on. Horizontal reach is the displacement of the origin.
Floor hitbox rule. Support is decided by the floor hitbox alone. A player can stand with floor_w − floor_overlap pixels of that box hanging past a ledge edge, and lands as soon as floor_overlap pixels of it touch the far platform. A gap of width G is therefore crossable when the simulated origin reach R satisfies:
R + floor_w − 2 × floor_overlap ≥ G
The term floor_w − 2 × floor_overlap is the floor bonus b (22 px at the baseline values). The generator and the validator both apply it, together with a 16 px safety margin on each side of every band (§8.4, Appendix B).
Derived traversal envelope. Reach figures are the physics derivation at the baseline constants (b = 22 px). Coyote time is part of the designed envelope — a jump leaving a ledge rides 0.10 s of carried speed — so the effective columns are the honest ones the validator enforces. Dodge chains assume the best dodge timing: because the dodge cancels all vertical momentum (§5.5), the longest reach comes from dodging about 0.22 s after the apex, with the fall already under way, and the simulation searches every logic frame for it (Appendix B).
