Jump is Space only. W and Up are exclusively attack-aim modifiers, so an up-slash can never jump and a jump can never eat an up-aim.
Shift / LT is the shared dodge-sprint button. The press fires a dodge on the press frame (zero added latency) whenever the dodge is available (stamina of 20 or more, cooldown clear). If the button is still held when the 6 f burst ends, the player flows into sprint; releasing earlier leaves a plain dodge. If the dodge is unavailable, a held press starts a plain sprint at once (15 stamina). In the air the press is an air dodge only. Full rules: §5.5.
The attack button is tap/hold too. Tap swings the sword — it fires on the press frame with zero added latency (the common action never waits on a hold timer). Holding past the 9 f threshold (three sprite frames, §5.8) chains the swing into the heavy weapon wind-up. The dedicated Heavy Weapon binding (F on keyboard, LB on gamepad — the one unassigned default) is freed; it returns as an accessibility remap (§12.3). Dodge works on the ground and in the air.
Guard roots the player. While guard is held the player cannot move or jump (§5.4); facing can still be changed in place, and dodge is the exit.
Attack from guard. Pressing attack while guard is held — including during a parry stun — cancels guard immediately and starts the swing. Guard must be freshly pressed afterwards: holding the button never re-raises it (§5.4).
Guard from attack. A guard press cancels a sword swing during its recovery frames only. A guard press is ignored during a heavy weapon commitment, which nothing can cancel (§5.4, §5.8).
Ignored inputs. During a heavy weapon commitment every input is ignored — movement, jump, dodge, guard, sword taps, and the attack button itself (releasing it does nothing). Presses are not buffered, except for presses in the final 2 frames of recovery, which carry over so the next action feels immediate.
All action inputs (jump, dodge, attack, Phase Slash, Freeze, grenade, guard) carry a 2 f pressed-buffer: a press that lands between input samples is registered on the next frame and consumed there — no press is ever eaten by the 30 Hz sample grid (§16).
Rebinding: full keyboard/mouse remapping UI in M2; gamepad presets in M2 and full pad remapping in M3 (§15).
Supported devices: keyboard + mouse; XInput, DualShock, and Switch Pro controllers.
4.4 Checkpoints, saves, and the death flow
obj_checkpoint obelisks: the generator places one at level entry and every 5th main-path chunk (about 3-4 per level). Touching one: full heal, grenade resupply, heavy weapon swap menu (§5.8), autosave.
Heal precedence rules:
O1. Completing the Overtime State always sets HP to exactly 35.
O2. If the completing event is a checkpoint touch, that checkpoint's full heal is suppressed — the save and grenade resupply still apply.
O3. If the completing event is the level exit, the next level's entry checkpoint full-heals as normal: a new level is a fresh start, and the 35 HP rule governs mid-level continuation, boss arenas, and exit-adjacent combat. (Deliberate: exiting under Overtime already banks the level.)
Save system:
Path: game_save_id — the per-game local storage directory — not working_directory (which is for packaged assets such as templates.json). Files: save.json + save.bak.
Write pattern (deliberately not rename-based — the manual does not guarantee that file_rename overwrites an existing destination, and it returns false on failure): (1) copy the current save.json to save.bak; (2) write the new save.json; (3) verify by re-reading and parsing; on any load-time parse failure, fall back to save.bak.
Contents: global.run (§11.5 — including level_seed and the persisted overtime{} state) plus a version field with migration hooks.
Autosave triggers: checkpoint touch, boss victory or arena clear, Afterlife transitions, level transition, and Overtime entry.
Save & Quit during Overtime: persists the full Overtime state — remaining timer, kill count, HP 0. Reloading resumes at the last checkpoint still in Overtime with the saved remaining time. There is no path by which quitting converts an Overtime into a free heal or dodges the death counter.
No manual saves; "Save & Quit" from the pause menu resumes at the last checkpoint.
