5.4 Guard & perfect parry [IMPLEMENTED — rework verified in M1b]
Guard: ground-only. The player cannot move while guard is held: hsp is forced to 0 and jump input is ignored. Facing can still be changed by pressing the opposite direction (turn in place). Dodge is the exit (it is usable from guard); releasing guard restores movement. Each blocked hit drains 10 stamina; at 0 stamina the guard breaks (0.5 s stagger, no damage). Stamina regen (§5.6) refuels turtling slowly, and the shared stamina pool means turtling disarms sprinting and dodging — the physical economy all trades against itself.
Hold Guard to block MELEE, ION, and BONE damage from the front; blocked hits apply pushback but no damage.
Blockable vs unblockable: SHOCKWAVE-class hits (boss stomps, slams) break guard — the hit is negated but the player staggers and guard drops. HEAVY, HARPOON, PUNCH, EXPLOSIVE, PHASE, and LASER are unblockable — full damage through guard. Enemy heavy attacks and boss kicks must be dodged (§5.5, §7.2).
Perfect parry: raising guard from neutral within 0.10 s (3 f) of an incoming hit negates the damage at zero stamina cost, stuns the attacker for 2.0 s, and resets its poise (§6.4); parried ion bolts reflect at 1.5x speed. The parry window is exactly one gameplay animation frame (1 sprite frame = 3 logic frames, §11.3) — what the player sees is precisely what is active. The window was sized with the 33 ms sampling slack included: the player-perceived acceptance is 100-133 ms.
Guard and attack cancels:
Attack cancels guard. Pressing attack while guard is held — including during a parry stun — cancels guard immediately and starts the swing (and, if the button stays held, the heavy chain). Guard must be freshly pressed afterwards; holding the guard button never re-raises it.
Guard cancels sword recovery. A guard press cancels a sword swing during its recovery frames only (sprite frames S3–S4, f7–f12). Startup and active frames cannot be cancelled, so a swing's hitbox always lives its full 3 f. This mirrors the dodge, which is also usable from sword recovery.
Cancel-raised guard has no parry window. A guard raised by cancelling a sword recovery blocks normally from its first frame but offers no perfect parry. The 3 f parry window exists only on a guard raised from neutral (idle, walk, sprint, or after the previous action has fully ended). Without this rule the sword's 6 f recovery would cost nothing against blockable attacks, and a swing-cancel-parry loop would fish for parries every cycle.
Nothing cancels a heavy commitment. Guard input is ignored from heavy wind-up start through recovery end, exactly like every other input (§5.8).
Ignored is not buffered, except for presses in the final 2 frames of recovery (§4.3).
Overtime ruling: blocked and parried hits never count as damage taken — a turtling player during Overtime delays but cannot escape the timer, and unguardable sources (heavy attacks, explosives, lasers, grabs) remain lethal (§5.1).
Anti-turtle bookkeeping is guard's shadow, not parry's: enemy heavy-attack triggers count plain blocks only — a parry already stuns the attacker for 2.0 s and resets its aggression (§7.2). Skilled play is rewarded, never punished, by the trigger.
5.5 Dodge & Sprint [PLANNED — M1b]
One button, two behaviors: the press fires a dodge; holding through the burst continues into sprint. Every sprint start from rest therefore begins with a dodge burst whenever the dodge is available — a launch step with free i-frames.
