0.5 s between dodges, anchored to dodge end (the 6 f burst plus a 15 f gap before the next is accepted)
Surface
Ground and air. A ground dodge is a grounded state: if the player leaves the ground during the burst, gravity resumes on the first unsupported frame — the dodge continues as a dash and a normal fall, not a hover. An air dodge is a straight horizontal line that cancels all vertical momentum: vsp is set to 0 at dodge start, gravity is suspended for the 6 f, and the fall resumes afterwards from vsp = 0 — the pre-dodge vertical speed is not restored. A dodge during a rise ends the rise at once (it costs height); a dodge during a fall resets the fall speed (it buys airtime, which the §4.1 reach figures include at the best dodge timing). A dodge never gains height. One air dodge per airborne period, refreshed on landing; a ground dodge that carries the player off a ledge does not consume it
No coyote after a dodge ledge exit
Leaving the ground while a dodge is active grants no coyote time: jump input during the carry and the following fall is buffered (4 f) and consumed on landing, never accepted in the air. Coyote applies only to ledge exits by walking or sprinting. Combined with the end-anchored 0.5 s cooldown, a ledge dodge cannot chain into a jump or a second dodge to extend reach (Appendix B, CH6)
Momentum clamps
On jump takeoff, hsp clamps to 330 px/s (sprint speed) — a jump out of a dodge is exactly a sprint-jump, never a 480 px/s rocket (§4.1). On dodge exit, hsp clamps to 330 as well — the burst speed exists only inside the 6 f
Cancels
Dodge is usable from guard and from sword recovery; it is ignored during a heavy weapon commitment (§5.8)

Sprint start (resolution order at the press frame):
Air: the press is an air dodge if one is available, otherwise a fizzle. There is no sprint in the air; takeoff speed carries per the 330 px/s clamp.
Ground, dodge available (stamina of 20 or more, cooldown clear): the dodge fires. If the button is still held when the burst ends and at least 15 stamina remains, sprint begins at 330 px/s (drain starts). With fewer than 15 remaining, the player walks and the button must be re-pressed. Releasing during the burst leaves a plain dodge.
Ground, dodge unavailable (cooldown active, or 15-19 stamina): a held press starts a plain sprint immediately (15 stamina to engage); releasing stops it. A rejected dodge at 15-19 stamina plays the fizzle cue; a cooldown rejection is silent. Below 15 stamina the press fizzles.
Re-engage after empty: after stamina hits 0, sprint requires 30 (anti-flutter).
After-images: during the dodge, the player trails 3 draw-only ghosts spawned at 2 f intervals, each living 6 f with alpha 0.5 / 0.3 / 0.15, drawn from the player sprite through the tint-shader path already built for hit flashes (§7.1). No collision, no logic — three cheap sprite draws, comfortably inside the §16 budget.
The air dodge's job: it is the mobility heir of the removed recoil jump — it extends jump reach (§4.1: walking 6 -> 10 tiles, sprinting 8 -> 12 with the floor bonus), answers enemy heavy attacks without waiting for the ground (§7.2), and recovers short jumps or a bad fall. It is deliberately not a hover or a climb: one per airtime, horizontal, and it never gains height.
Dodge design summary: i-frames full-duration; fires on press; pass-through on enemy bodies with an ejection rule; vertical momentum cancelled; horizontal momentum clamped at takeoff and exit so traversal bands stay honest; no coyote after a ledge dodge; cooldown anchored to the end of the move.
