The tap/hold contract:
Tap — LMB / X press swings the Hard Light Sword immediately, on the press frame, with zero added latency: the common action never waits on a hold timer.
Hold threshold (the point of no return) — 9 f (0.30 s, three sprite frames). If the button is still down at f9, the heavy commitment begins on the next sprite-frame boundary and the swing's remaining recovery is absorbed into it. 9 f is deliberately generous: deliberate taps run 100-150 ms, and at a 30 Hz sample grid a 150 ms tap occupies at most 5 held frames (167 ms) — over 130 ms clear of the threshold. Ordinary taps can never trip a heavy by accident (R18).
Hold — the strike executes automatically at the end of the wind-up; no further input is needed. Holding past the threshold is the commitment.
No abort. Once the commitment begins, releasing the attack button does nothing: no input cancels a heavy attack, at any point, from the first wind-up frame to the end of recovery.
Uninterruptible. Nothing external interrupts a heavy commitment. The player still takes damage from hits that land (and receives the normal 22 f of i-frames afterwards), but damage does not flinch, cancel, stagger, stun, or knock back the player; guard-break staggers, stun effects, and movement overrides (a harpoon's pull) are ignored, and a harpoon that connects deals its hit damage on contact without the pull. Because no knockback applies, the Wall-Slam Bonus cannot trigger. In Overtime a heavy commitment is an all-in: any damage taken still ends the run (§5.1).
From the air — heavy weapons are ground-only, but the hold persists: an air press swings the air melee, and if the button is still held on the first grounded frame, the commitment starts there. Jump in, plant, swing.
Before the armory (loadout.heavy_weapon == WEAPON.NONE): holding does nothing beyond the normal sword swing.
Common rules (the cost of the damage):
Locked in: from the first wind-up frame through the end of recovery the player is rooted (hsp forced to 0) and every input is ignored — dodge, guard, jump, movement, sword taps, and the attack button itself. Inputs are not buffered, except presses in the final 2 frames of recovery (§4.3). Because nothing can cancel or interrupt it, the locked-in time is exactly wind-up + swing/recovery.
Ground-only; horizontal, in the facing direction; no 4-way use.
All strikes are type MELEE (blocked by the Heavy Cyborg's shield, §6.2 — dodge pass-through, grenades, and Phase Slash are the shield answers); they deal multiplied poise damage.
The chain deals sword 18 + the heavy's damage — the swing that opens the commitment is a real hit (balance flag R16: if the chain overperforms in playtests, tuning.json folds the opening swing's damage into the heavy rather than re-spec'ing the input).
