6.4 Poise, stagger, stun [IMPLEMENTED]
Every enemy carries a poise meter (values in §7.2). Damage dealt to HP is also dealt to poise — heavy weapons deal multiplied poise damage (§5.8). Poise fully regenerates after 3 s without taking a hit.
Poise break causes a 1.2 s stagger (flash-white shader pulse — implemented). Heavy attacks are the exception: an enemy heavy attack cannot be interrupted, so a poise break reached during one is deferred and its stagger begins the moment the heavy attack's recovery ends. A frozen enemy takes no poise damage (§5.2).
Perfect parry (§5.4) causes a 2.0 s stun plus poise reset, regardless of remaining poise.
The mace shockwave (§5.8) causes a 1.0 s stun followed by 3.0 s of stun immunity for that enemy. Immunity applies only to the mace stun; it does not block parry stuns or poise breaks.
Bosses have no poise; they stagger only through designed mechanics (wall-slam stun, harpoon miss) which carry their own immunity windows (§10.1).
6.5 Knockback, i-frames, and continuous hazards
Standard knockback 220 px/s, decay 1200 px/s². Boss-class knockback (kicks) 520 px/s.
Player i-frames: 0.73 s (22 f) after any hit. Enemies have no i-frames — poise governs their reaction to pressure.
Continuous hazards — Modal Laser Beam, Dual Laser Sweep, and any future beam field: damage on contact, re-hit every 0.77 s (23 f) while touching (one frame past the i-frame window so re-hits always land), hard-capped at 2 hits per target per activation. A 4 s beam therefore deals at most 40 to a single player, not more; a full sweep at most 40. Discrete hits (bolts, slams, crossbones) are unaffected and use normal i-frames.
6.6 Telegraph standard [IMPLEMENTED as a pattern]
Every hostile attack has a telegraph of at least 0.4 s (12 f): pose change + flash (implemented shader) + audio cue. Per-enemy and per-boss windows are specified in §7.2 and §10.
Heavy attacks telegraph at least 0.8 s with a double-flash and a distinct audio thump — visibly and audibly different from normal attacks, and colorblind-safe (the cue is count + sound, not hue, §12.3).
Animation coupling: at the 10 fps gameplay animation clock, a 0.4 s telegraph is exactly 4 sprite frames — telegraph animations must be authored with at least 4 frames (bosses at 20 fps: at least 8). Every telegraph is a whole number of sprite frames; boss telegraphs are an even number of boss frames so they end on a logic-frame boundary (§11.3).
Telegraphs are not interruptible by player damage (except designed staggers). Heavy attacks are never interruptible — not by damage, poise break, stun, or knockback; a parry cannot touch them (they are unblockable); only a Time Freeze suspends one, and it resumes where it stopped. The designed outcome of a boss move (a wall-miss stun, a harpoon miss) is part of the move, not an interruption.
6.7 Sympathetic detonation limits
When an explosive detonates, other explosives inside its blast radius arm with a 3 f delay, then detonate.
Chain resolution is capped: max 4 detonations per frame (excess deferred to the next frame), max 8 detonations per chain; grenades beyond the cap become visual duds.
Budget rationale: §16 (performance).

