Design closure notes: death 4+ before the boss is beaten cannot occur (death 3 always enters the block); a death mid-block advances the attempt counter (T4/T6); there is a single taunt line between attempts, not a full dialogue; quit-mid-fight is covered by T13.
9.4 Permanent mode & the Echo Reaper
In REAPER_PERMANENT, every True Death skips dialogue and starts the Echo Reaper: Phase 1 kit only, 70% HP (560), single attempt (T8/T9). A plainly labeled retreat door is present in the arena; using it respawns the player at their checkpoint without penalty (the Reaper comments). This prevents the punishment spiral of "a struggling player must beat a hard optional boss on every death forever" while keeping the Reaper an unavoidable presence.
9.5 Death-counter semantics
global.run.deaths increments only on True Death (Overtime State failure or environmental instakill).
Entering Overtime State does not count. Completing Overtime State does not count (T12).
Dying inside any Afterlife fight consumes an attempt but never increments deaths — the counter tracks campaign deaths that drive the Reaper's escalation; the fight tracks its own attempts.
Persistence: part of global.run, saved and loaded with the run (§11.5). Persists across save/quit. Reset only by New Game.
9.6 Regression definition
level_index = max(1, level_index - 2), computed on the §8.1 list (boss arenas count).
The player keeps all abilities, upgrades, items, and heavy weapon choice — regression costs time, not power (deliberate).
The target level reloads at its entry checkpoint; its enemies and pickups reset per normal level-re-entry rules.
Boss-arena targets: if the regressed-to level is a boss arena whose boss is already defeated, the arena is entered in its cleared state — the boss does not re-fight; a transit corridor leads through to the exit. Unlocked progression doors (the Carrier Key bulkhead at L8) never re-lock.
9.7 Reward & acknowledgement
Victory (T5/T7/T10) grants the Reaper's Toll: +25 max HP (100 to 125), the Soul Scythe cosmetic reskin for the Hard Light Sword (found embedded where the Reaper fell), Afterlife permanently closed, and the run's end screen gains the line "Deaths cheated: N". A hidden boss with no acknowledgement is a missed feature — this closes that gap.
9.8 Hidden-system risk (accepted, mitigated)
Design risk, called out explicitly: players do not know the rules (2-attempt block, regression, permanent mode). Some will read regression as a punishing bug. This opacity is intentional — it is the Afterlife's identity — and the dialogue teaches the rules at the moments they bind:
D1 establishes that deaths are being counted ("I'll be counting").
D2 warns that the third time is different.
The mid-block taunt states the 2-attempt rule verbatim — "Once more. There is no third." — so no player can finish the block without having been told it exists.
The post-regression line states the new permanent regime verbatim.
Further mitigations: the retreat door in permanent mode; assist options (§12.3); patch-note transparency outside the game.
Sample dialogue (final lines, subject to editing):
D1: "Everyone gets one free pass. Enjoy the walk back. I'll be counting."
D2: "Again. You are spending my patience — and it is the last currency you want to spend. The third time will not be a conversation."
D3: "Third time. No more pleasantries." (transforms)
Taunt between attempts: "Once more. There is no third."
Post-regression (permanent mode): "You had your two chances. From now on, every fall answers to me."
On victory: "...Fine. Keep your hours." (the scythe stays behind)
On retreat: "Coward's exit. It will be here when you fall again."
9.9 Persistence & edge cases
State is written at every transition (T1-T11); see T13 for quit-mid-fight.
Quitting during a dialogue: on reload the dialogue is skipped and replaced by a one-line reminder; the state has already advanced.
Afterlife fights pause deaths but consume attempts; the Overtime State cannot trigger inside the Afterlife (§5.1).
New Game calls scr_run_reset() — all Afterlife state returns to LURKING.

