Dual Laser Sweep — geometry-first design. Beams cannot be jumped; the redesign guarantees safe lanes by geometry in the 576 px arena:
Both hands lock to the ceiling and emit two vertical beams (24 px wide), starting touching each other at the arena's center line.
0-2.8 s — sweep out: the beams separate at 80 px/s each (center 288 px to 64 px-from-wall = 224 px of travel = 2.80 s), halting 2 tiles (64 px) from each wall.
2.8-5.2 s — hold: the beams sit near the walls; the entire center is safe.
5.2-8.0 s — sweep back: the beams return to center and the sweep ends (2.8 + 2.4 + 2.8 = 8.0 s).
Safe lanes, guaranteed: at least 64 px at both walls at all times, plus the whole center once the beams separate (the gap opens at 160 px/s). Crossing through a beam is possible with Phase Slash i-frames (6 f covers the 24 px transit) but never required.
Damage: continuous hazard — max 2 hits per activation per §6.5.
Counterattack window: both hands are committed to the ceiling for the full 8 s, so the Body and Head are the targets (stated explicitly).
Figure-8 integrity & Head reachability — pinned numbers. The Body's path amplitude is clamped to stay at least 2 tiles above the highest platform — it never intersects terrain. The hands ignore terrain by design: they are background-layer threats with their own hitboxes; the player cannot body-block or be pushed by them, and their tracking is telegraphed by the pre-slam pause. The Head hangs beneath the Body's forward mass (a dangling weak point). Reach is restored by geometry, not player tech: the Figure-8's lower lobe dips to 3.5 tiles (112 px) above the highest platform at least once per loop (about 12 s), and two standable bone outcrops (1.5 tiles high, authored into the arena under the two lower-lobe points — also listed in the §10.5 arena table) raise the effective floor beneath them.
Reach check, with its reference points defined (§4.1). The target is the bottom edge of the Head's hitbox, measured from the surface the player stands on (the outcrop top). The player's reach is the origin's jump rise (98 px) plus the up-slash hitbox, which extends 48 px above the top of the collision box (§5.8). The check is deliberately conservative: it ignores the height of the collision box itself, which adds to the real reach. Conservative reach 98 + 48 = 146 px against a 112 px target — a 34 px margin (about one tile), larger in practice by the box height. The frame-alignment audit verifies that the authored up-slash hitbox extends the full 48 px, and AC22 enforces the 15 s window.
