Priority: PAUSE > DIALOGUE > HITSTOP > FROZEN > RUN. Ad-hoc pause flags are deprecated; every subsystem reads scr_time_state(). An object's anim_tick (§11.3) advances only in the states where its owner ticks, so animation-bound combat windows pause and resume with their owner and never drift across a hitstop or a freeze.
The unskippable freeze gate. The parent owns the only Step event in the hostile tree and calls an overridable on_step(); a child that forgets a check cannot exist, because children do not write Step events at all:
// obj_freezable — Create
on_step = function () {};                        // children override this in their Create
mem_image_speed = image_speed;                   // mem_* ALWAYS exist
mem_speed       = speed;
mem_path_speed  = path_speed;
if (global.time_frozen) {                        // instances born during a freeze spawn pre-frozen
    image_speed = 0;  speed = 0;  path_speed = 0;
}


// obj_freezable — Step (the ONLY Step event in the entire hostile tree)
if (global.time_frozen) exit;
on_step();
Event restrictions for hostile objects, enforced in code review: no Step, no Begin/End Step, no Collision events, no Alarms (§11.3). All gameplay logic — including collision handling, which is already manual sweeps per §11.1 — lives in on_step(). This closes the "Begin/End Step and Collision aren't gated" hole because those events simply do not exist on hostiles.
Freeze bookkeeping (idempotent).
function scr_freeze_set(_on) {
    if (_on == global.time_frozen) return;       // idempotent — no double-apply, no lost mem_*
    global.time_frozen = _on;
    with (obj_freezable) {
        if (_on) { mem_image_speed = image_speed;  image_speed = 0;
                   mem_speed       = speed;        speed       = 0;
                   mem_path_speed  = path_speed;   path_speed  = 0; }
        else     { image_speed = mem_image_speed;  speed = mem_speed;  path_speed = mem_path_speed; }
    }
    // Gameplay particle systems and gameplay sequences are updated manually
    // in their owners' on_step (already gated). Ambient layers keep running.
}
Because mem_* are initialized in the parent Create and the function early-outs on repeat calls, objects created during a freeze restore correctly when it ends (their Create captured the intended speeds before zeroing them).
Remaining rule: player grenades are not frozen (the player is operational) and detonate on schedule, but their blasts deal half damage to frozen enemies (§5.2); frozen enemies take frozen_damage_mult (0.5) from every player source, resolved in the hit pipeline rather than in on_step(), so a frozen enemy can still die and be removed; boss freeze budgets (§5.2) are enforced by the scheduler, not by the freeze system.
Migration task (M1a): existing enemy and projectile Step bodies move into on_step() methods. The replay harness (§17) plus ACs 9-10 are the regression net (risk R13).
11.3 Timers, alarms, and the animation driver [STANDARD]
Alarms are forbidden in hostile objects: the runtime decrements them regardless of Step bypasses, so telegraphs, wind-ups, and cooldowns would keep ticking through a freeze. All hostile timing uses per-object frame counters or timer structs:
windup = timer_new(12, function () { state = ATTACK; });  // 0.4 s at 30 fps
The animation driver (frame-perfect by construction). Gameplay sprites animate at 10 fps and boss sprites at 20 fps, but the animation is driven from an integer counter, not by image_speed. image_speed stays 0 on every combat sprite; each object increments its own anim_tick once per logic step in which it is allowed to tick (§11.2), and derives the displayed frame from it:
// every object with combat animation — runs inside the object's gated step
anim_tick += 1;
image_speed = 0;
image_index = (anim_tick * ANIM_FPS) div LOGIC_HZ;   // ANIM_FPS = 10 (gameplay) or 20 (boss)
// gameplay: 3 ticks per frame.  boss: floor(anim_tick * 2/3) — frame lengths alternate 2,1,2,1 ticks
When a sprite changes, anim_tick resets to 0. Looping animations wrap with a modulo on the frame count. Because image_index is always an integer derived by integer arithmetic, there is no float accumulation and the sprite can never drift from its hitbox by a logic frame.
Combat windows are whole sprite frames. At the 10 fps gameplay clock, one sprite frame is exactly 3 logic frames, so every combat window — hitbox active spans, the parry window, telegraphs, heavy wind-ups, swing timelines — is a whole number of sprite frames (3 f, 6 f, 9 f...). Boss sprites run one frame per 1.5 logic frames, so boss windows are an even number of boss frames (a multiple of 3 logic frames) and always end on a logic-frame boundary. The authored value is the sprite-frame range; the live check is exact:
function scr_hb_live(_sprite, _a, _b) {
    return sprite_index == _sprite && image_index >= _a && image_index <= _b;
}
Per-frame hitbox data. Hitbox rectangles live in a data file (hitboxes.json, an Included File) keyed by sprite name and sprite frame: for every attack frame, the rectangles that are live on that frame. scr_hitbox_bound (§6.1) spawns an obj_hitbox from the entry when its owner's sprite frame enters the bound range and removes it when the frame leaves it, so what the player sees is exactly what is active — "frame-perfect" is a data property, not a timing hope. Free-running timers remain legal only for non-visual cooldowns (attack cooldowns, regen delays, the dodge cooldown) and non-animated hazards (§6.1). The dodge is a movement state and is timer-driven by design (its i-frame window is the state's lifetime, 6 f = 2 sprite frames).
Audits (standing deliverables): (1) the asset audit walks every sprite used by a combat object and flags any object whose animation is not driven by anim_tick or whose ANIM_FPS is not its class constant (AC31); (2) the frame-alignment audit checks that every attack sprite has hitbox data for exactly its authored active frames, that each active window equals N sprite frames x ticks-per-frame, that boss windows are even boss-frame counts, and that the sword's up-slash rectangle extends 48 px above the collision box (AC36). The dodge after-images (§5.5) are draw-only ghosts and are exempt (they animate by alpha). The typewriter and all dialogue timing live in the dialogue controller, which is not freezable — dialogue is explicitly exempt from Time Freeze (FROZEN row in §11.2).
11.4 Dialogue system [PARTIALLY IMPLEMENTED — controller refactor in M1a]
obj_dialogue_controller (persistent) owns all dialogue: a queue of requests { speaker, lines[], on_complete }. NPC and boss objects only submit requests — which solves the problem of the Reaper's final line needing to survive the NPC to boss Phase 1 object swap.
The controller is also the single place that pauses gameplay during dialogue (via the time-domain manager, DIALOGUE state).
Typewriter: 30 characters/s, own Step counters; skip on input; auto-advance 1.2 s after a completed line.
Rendering: Draw GUI on the controller (decoupled from camera zoom). Backplates are draw_set_alpha + draw_rectangle with alpha and color reset afterward — no surface management is involved or needed.
All lines live in one data script (scr_dialogue_data), keyed by enum.
