11.6 Tuning data [STANDARD — JSON migration in M1a]
Every number in Appendix A is a tuning value. Baseline home: a scr_config constants struct. In M1a the numbers move to a tuning.json Included File loaded into global.cfg at boot, so balancing requires no code changes.
Timings are stored as seconds or sprite frames and converted at load. Each combat timing is authored in seconds or in sprite frames and converted to logic frames with LOGIC_HZ; the loader asserts that every converted window is an integer number of logic frames and, for animation-bound windows, a whole number of sprite frames (even boss-frame counts for bosses). Changing LOGIC_HZ therefore re-derives every frame count instead of requiring a re-port (§16).
Floor hitbox constants floor_w and floor_overlap live in tuning.json beside the physics constants; the traversal validator reads them (R21).
Hitbox data (hitboxes.json, §11.3) is a sibling Included File.
Appendix A becomes a generated artifact: a small script renders the appendix markdown from tuning.json, run at commit time (or in CI); the body of this document keeps intent and rules, not duplicate numbers, and the appendix header says "GENERATED — do not edit". Until the generator lands in M1a, the appendix is hand-maintained and the data file wins any dispute. The traversal validator (§8.4, Appendix B) reads tuning.json directly — the same single source of truth feeds the physics, the appendix, and the reachability simulation.
11.7 Level pipeline engine rules
See §8.4 "Engine compatibility rules" (runtime-only export; Included File registration; true tile layers; scr_tilemap_fill shim; object_get_sprite lookups) and the template schema (§8.4). These are permanent standards, not one-off fixes.
11.8 Parallax background system [IMPLEMENTED]
Three-layer desert parallax with horizontal-only wrapping (vertical looping was removed to kill seam artifacts). Per-layer speed ratios 0.15 / 0.40 / 0.75 of camera x. Layers are configured via asset_get_index lookups so layer and sprite names are data, not hardcoded references. Acts 2-3 get their own three-layer sets (§13).
11.9 Projectile variants — spawn parameters, not "pooling" [STANDARD]
Variants are spawn-parameter projectile variants, passed at creation via the var_struct argument of instance_create_layer. Per the GameMaker manual, struct variables are applied to the new instance after its Variable Definitions but before its Create event runs — which also means a Create that unconditionally assigns the same variable would silently overwrite the injected value. Defaults are therefore guarded:
// obj_crossbone — Create
if (!variable_instance_exists(id, "bounce_enabled"))   bounce_enabled = true;
if (!variable_instance_exists(id, "shatter_on_solid")) shatter_on_solid = false;
if (!variable_instance_exists(id, "max_bounces"))      max_bounces = 5;


// spawner — Crossbone Stream variant
instance_create_layer(x, y, "Projectiles", obj_crossbone, {
    bounce_enabled:   false,
    shatter_on_solid: true,
    max_bounces:      0,
    damage:           12
});
True pooling (deactivate and reuse) is unnecessary at this scale: the global hostile-projectile cap is 64 instances (§16), which instance creation handles comfortably.

