12.2 Feedback standards
Hit-flash shader (implemented), 2 f hitstop on melee connect, toggleable screenshake, freeze vignette (desaturation + border frost — visually distinct from the damage flash), poise-break pulse on enemy stagger, a stun icon over mace-stunned enemies, dodge after-images (§5.5), a pre-commit cue for heavy weapons (weapon glint plus the first note of the audio ramp at f6, three frames before the point of no return, §5.8), and rejected-input feedback: a dodge without stamina or a Freeze below full energy fizzles with a distinct sound and an icon flash — the player always learns why nothing happened (§12.3 colorblind rule: the cue is sound + icon, not hue). Hits taken during a heavy commitment play the hit sound and flash without a flinch animation, so the uninterruptible state reads as armor rather than as ignored damage (§5.8). Frozen enemies hit during a Time Freeze show a half-strength hit flash. Heavy wind-ups carry a rising audio ramp (the player-side counterpart of the §6.6 telegraph standard) so a held attack announces itself.
12.3 Accessibility
Remappable keyboard/mouse (M2); gamepad presets (M2) and full remapping (M3).
Screenshake and flash-intensity sliders (the flash shader's intensity is a scalar).
Colorblind safety: projectiles are shape-coded, not just color-coded — enemy ion bolts are diamonds, bones are cross-shaped, grenades are round; the freeze tint differs from the damage tint; the heavy-attack telegraph is a double flash + audio thump (count and sound, not hue).
Text speed and size options for dialogue.
Hold/toggle options for guard (the shared dodge-sprint button fires a dodge on press and sprints on hold; the attack button's hold branch is the heavy weapon). A dedicated-key option for heavy weapons (the freed F / LB binding) is a Should-list item for players who cannot hold reliably — the hold chain stays the default.
Assist mode (optional, clearly labeled): Extended Overtime (45 s), Reaper Mercy (§10.4). No hidden assist consequences.

13. Art & Audio Direction (summary)
32 px grid pixel art; the player silhouette keeps the highest contrast against every biome palette (desert ochre, carrier teal-steel, homeworld bone-violet).
Art is authored for the 640 x 360 base (20 x 11 tile view); the player silhouette is re-validated at this scale before slot art hardens (R12, M1a exit demo). Boss arenas ship with the 4 px frame trim (§10.5) as part of their frame art.
Animation clocks: gameplay sprites animate at 10 fps, boss sprites at 20 fps — boss reads as "more alive" than the world by design. Sprite frame counts are authored to the clocks (a 0.4 s telegraph = 4 gameplay frames), every combat window is a whole number of sprite frames, and every attack frame has a matching hitbox entry (§11.3).
Heavy weapon silhouettes: greatsword reads wide, spear reads long, mace reads heavy — each identifiable at 640 x 360 zoom and in the after-image trail (dodge ghosts use the current pose, so weapon swaps stay legible mid-fight). The wind-up pose gets a rising audio ramp so a held attack announces itself and reads as intent, not lag.
Shape language: everything hostile is angular; everything player-owned is rounded (reinforces the colorblind shape coding).
Three parallax layer sets, one per act (framework implemented, §11.8).
Audio: per-act ambience plus a combat intensity layer; a standardized 0.4 s telegraph cue shared by all enemies (per-variant pitch variants); heavy attacks use the double-thump variant (§6.6); the Reaper's theme is the main theme detuned and reversed. Music ducks during dialogue.

14. Narrative & Teaching Structure
Act 1 (Desert): home, abduction aftermath. Teaches the core kit (level table, §8.1; set-piece injection, §8.4). Traefin is a cyborg duelist left to guard the abductees' trail.
Act 2 (Carrier): infiltration. The armory after Traefin's arena presents the three heavy weapons on pedestals — the survivor takes one relic and learns to plant their feet (§5.8); Phase Slash is taken from the same breached armory at L6's entry; Time Freeze is salvaged from the engine core at L7's exit. Mandaxx is the carrier's warden; its escape (radio stinger) sets up Act 3, and the Carrier Key it drops opens the Detention Ring bulkhead at L8's entry — the locked door holding the abductees' prison blocks.
Act 3 (Homeworld): the rescue. Modal is the species' final instrument. L11 teaches stamina management across long traversals (§8.1).
The Reaper is the only NPC with dialogue (§9.8 lines) — by design: the campaign speaks through environment and radio, the Afterlife through him.
The ability unlock schedule is data in global.run.loadout (§11.5); each unlock is paired with a safe practice stretch in the same level via its set-piece chunk (§8.4). The teaching order is load-bearing: nothing is taught before its unlock exists (§8.1 — heavy-weapon practice begins at L6, where the armory is).

