16. Non-Functional Requirements
Requirement
Specification
Resolution & aspect
Base 640 x 360 (16:9), integer-scales cleanly to 720p (x2), 1080p (x3), 1440p (x4), 4K (x6) and the Steam Deck's 1280 x 800 (x2, 40 px letterbox bars top and bottom)
Scaling
Integer-first: scale by the largest whole factor and letterbox the remainder; a hybrid filter (integer pre-scale + linear remainder) is the documented fallback only if a future target demands fill
Framerate
30 fps locked logic (LOGIC_HZ 30, room speed 30); vsync on; no unlocked-fps mode; frame pacing validated on 60 Hz and 144 Hz displays (M4, R20). Physics integrates with dt = 1 / LOGIC_HZ and every timing is stored in seconds or sprite frames and converted at load (§11.6), so the logic rate is a constant, not a port
Input model
30 Hz input sampling with a 2 f pressed-buffer on all action inputs — no press is eaten between samples; the 3 f parry window includes the sampling slack by design (perceived 100-133 ms); hold thresholds are expressed in sampled frames. 30 fps is a feel and battery decision, not a performance constraint. Two separate contingencies, only if M4 feel tests fail: (a) input latency (R19) — keep 30 Hz logic and sample and timestamp presses at 60 Hz, a change confined to the input layer; (b) display pacing (R20) — set LOGIC_HZ to 60. Because timings are authored in seconds and whole sprite frames and converted at load, (b) re-derives every frame count (a gameplay sprite frame becomes 6 logic frames, a boss frame 3) and is verified by the replay harness; per-frame hitbox data is keyed by sprite frame and is unaffected
Animation
Gameplay sprites 10 fps; boss sprites 20 fps, both driven from an integer anim_tick (§11.3); combat windows are whole sprite frames (boss windows even boss-frame counts); audited by the asset audit and the frame-alignment audit (AC31, AC36)
Min spec
i5-8400 / GTX 1050-class / 8 GB RAM, 1080p
Performance budgets
At the 33.4 ms frame: Step 8 ms or less; draw 12 ms or less; no frame above 33.4 ms during Modal grenade spew / bullet hail / detonation chains on min spec — measured on YYC builds, not the VM; the replay harness drives the worst-case scenarios deterministically
Instance budgets
64 hostile projectiles alive max (hard cap; spawner requests beyond it are dropped and logged); 250 active gameplay instances max; 4 detonations/frame max (§6.7); 3 dodge after-image ghosts max (draw-only)
Save behavior
Single slot; JSON at game_save_id (§4.4); copy-to-.bak + write + verify; no rename-based atomicity (the manual does not guarantee file_rename overwrites an existing destination); version field with migration hooks; autosave-only
Toolchain
IDE + runtime version pinned and recorded in the repo README; the current runtime predates tilemap_fill (§8.4 shims) — the upgrade-or-stay decision is an M1a action item (R11); several doc rules (var_struct ordering, layer behavior) are version-dependent, so a runtime upgrade re-runs the §8.4 checks
Platforms
Windows 10+ first; Steam Deck verification target; consoles are a target (specific platforms to be chosen) and the game is designed console-accessible from the start: controller-first input and menus, no mouse-only or hover-only interaction, title-safe HUD, platform-safe save I/O behind a thin wrapper, suspend/resume and controller-disconnect handling, and 30 fps budgets measured on YYC builds; Mac/Linux post-1.0
Input devices
KB/M + XInput / DualShock / Switch Pro; the gamepad is a first-class device — every gameplay action and every menu is operable on a controller alone (AC42); no always-online features; fully offline single player


